\title{Drag on Smooth Circular Cylinders Towed Through a Water Tank at Moderate Reynolds Numbers}
\author{[Author names and affiliations to be added]}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The mean drag on three nominally smooth circular cylinders was measured in a towing tank over nominal Reynolds numbers from approximately $5.1\times10^3$ to $5.6\times10^4$. Cylinders of diameter 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm were towed at speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s$^{-1}$, with a nominal 0.60 m wetted length. A load cell recorded force during each tow; the force was obtained by subtracting a pre-run tare and applying a calibration sensitivity. For each run, the mean force over the 3--8 s interval was used with the projected area $DL$ to calculate the drag coefficient. The existing analysis gives mean $C_D$ values of 1.144, 1.153, and 1.178 for the 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm cylinders, respectively. Within each size series, $C_D$ shows no strong monotonic dependence on Reynolds number over the tested range. These measurements are consistent with an approximately constant subcritical cylinder drag coefficient, but they do not establish a diameter effect: each diameter was tested in a separate campaign, and the campaigns differed in water temperature and load-cell mounting/calibration. The estimate also uses water properties at 20 °C, has not been corrected for tank blockage or end and free-surface effects, and includes one flagged 50.8 mm run with possible tare drift. The results are therefore presented as a preliminary towing-tank dataset and a basis for a controlled repeat campaign.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} circular cylinder; drag coefficient; towing tank; Reynolds number; force measurement

\section{Introduction}
Flow past a circular cylinder is a canonical bluff-body problem. Its wake and force response vary with Reynolds number, and the wake undergoes changes in organization as Reynolds number increases \citep{Williamson1996}. Reference measurements on long smooth cylinders show that force and pressure characteristics in cross-flow have been studied over broad Reynolds-number ranges, while also making clear that test conditions and model geometry matter when comparing drag data \citep{Achenbach1968}.

This study uses a small towing tank and load-cell force measurement to determine the mean drag on three smooth circular cylinders at model-scale speeds. The immediate question is whether the measured drag coefficient is approximately constant across the Reynolds-number range accessible to the apparatus, and whether the three tested diameters give comparable values. The dataset comprises thirty single tows: ten speeds for each of three diameters. Because cylinder size is confounded with test date, water temperature, and (for the March campaign) a remounted load cell and revised data-acquisition format, comparisons among diameters are descriptive rather than a controlled test of scale effects.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Test arrangement and run matrix}
Tests were conducted in a towing tank reported in the laboratory notes as 0.9 m wide and 0.75 m deep. Smooth circular cylinders with nominal diameters of 25.4, 38.1, and 50.8 mm were tested with a nominal 0.60 m submerged length. The run log lists ten commanded speeds from 0.2 to 1.1 m s$^{-1}$ in 0.1 m s$^{-1}$ increments for each cylinder. The 25.4 mm cylinder was tested on 14 October 2025 in water at 16.9--17.6 °C; the 50.8 mm cylinder was tested on 3 November 2025 at 15.0--15.6 °C; and the 38.1 mm cylinder was tested on 12 March 2026 at 13.7--14.3 °C. The actual water-temperature values were logged per run. Detailed cylinder support geometry, clearances to tank boundaries, surface roughness, and tow-speed calibration are not recorded in the files reviewed for this draft and should be added before submission.

\subsection{Force measurement and reduction}
The load cell was calibrated by hanging known masses and fitting voltage as a linear function of force. The September 2025 calibration gave a sensitivity of approximately 0.020 V N$^{-1}$ in the original processing notes; a new mounting and calibration in March 2026 gave approximately 0.0213 V N$^{-1}$. For each run, voltage recorded before 1 s was averaged to form the tare. The force history was calculated as
\begin{equation}
  F(t)=\frac{V(t)-\overline{V}_{t<1\,\mathrm{s}}}{S},
\end{equation}
where $S$ is the campaign sensitivity. The reported run mean and temporal standard deviation were evaluated over $3<t<8$ s. This standard deviation describes variation within one tow; it is not an estimate of between-run repeatability. The October and November data were recorded as comma-separated files and processed with the September calibration; the March data used semicolon-separated files and the March sensitivity. Run 17 (50.8 mm cylinder, 0.8 m s$^{-1}$) is marked in the log as having a possibly anomalous post-run zero. It is retained in the reported analysis, but its influence is noted below.

The drag coefficient and Reynolds number were calculated using
\begin{equation}
  C_D=\frac{\overline{F}}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho U^2 D L}, \qquad
  Re_D=\frac{UD}{\nu},
\end{equation}
where $U$ is the logged tow speed, $D$ is cylinder diameter, and $L=0.60$ m is the nominal wetted length. The notebook used $\rho=998$ kg m$^{-3}$ and $\nu=1.004\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$ s$^{-1}$, corresponding to water at 20 °C, for every campaign. Consequently, the Reynolds numbers below are nominal values and should be recomputed using the logged temperatures in a final analysis. No blockage, support, free-surface, or end-effect correction was applied. Based on the nominal projected area $DL$ and tank cross-section $0.9\times0.75$ m$^2$, the simple area blockage ratio is approximately 2.3--4.5% across the tested diameters; this estimate does not include supports or local boundary effects.

\section{Results}
The nominal Reynolds-number ranges and mean drag coefficients from the notebook are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:summary}. The full dataset is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cd}. Within each campaign, the coefficients fluctuate around a roughly level value rather than displaying a strong monotonic trend with speed. The mean values range from 1.144 to 1.178 across size series. The within-series standard deviations in Table~\ref{tab:summary} are calculated across the ten rounded per-run coefficients and therefore combine measurement scatter and any systematic trend; they are not uncertainty intervals on the means.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Summary of the existing analysis. Reynolds-number bounds use $\nu$ at 20 °C. The standard deviation is across the ten per-run $C_D$ values in each size series.}
\label{tab:summary}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Cylinder diameter & $Re_D$ range & Mean $C_D$ & SD of $C_D$ & Number of runs \\
\midrule
25.4 mm & $5.06\times10^3$--$2.78\times10^4$ & 1.144 & 0.032 & 10 \\
38.1 mm & $7.59\times10^3$--$4.17\times10^4$ & 1.153 & 0.026 & 10 \\
50.8 mm & $1.01\times10^4$--$5.57\times10^4$ & 1.178 & 0.070 & 10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The elevated value for run 17 is visible in the 50.8 mm series ($C_D=1.357$). Excluding that run solely as a sensitivity check changes the 50.8 mm series mean from 1.178 to 1.159. The log's drift note is not sufficient by itself to establish that this measurement is invalid, so the primary summary retains it. A repeat tow and inspection of the raw pre-run and post-run zero levels are needed to decide how it should be treated.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{../figures/cd_vs_re.png}
\caption{Drag coefficient versus nominal Reynolds number from the current notebook analysis. Reynolds numbers use water properties at 20 °C. Series correspond to different test dates and load-cell campaigns.}
\label{fig:cd}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{../figures/timeseries_run005.png}
\caption{Example processed force history for run 5 (25.4 mm cylinder, 0.6 m s$^{-1}$). The shaded interval indicates the 3--8 s window used to calculate the run mean.}
\label{fig:trace}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:trace} illustrates the force reduction for one representative run. The existing processing applies a single pre-run tare and a fixed campaign calibration; it does not fit a drift correction or quantify uncertainty in the calibration slope.

\section{Discussion}
The near-constant values observed within each size series are consistent with the broad subcritical regime of circular-cylinder cross-flow, where drag does not vary sharply over modest Reynolds-number intervals \citep{Achenbach1968,Williamson1996}. The present measurements do not resolve a reliable dependence on diameter: the means differ by only about 3\%, but the campaigns were not performed under matched conditions. In particular, all cylinders were tested at different water temperatures and dates, and the load cell was remounted and recalibrated before the 38.1 mm campaign. Those differences prevent attributing the mean offsets to cylinder diameter.

Several systematic effects should be resolved before using these coefficients as reference data. First, the actual water temperature should be used to calculate $\nu$ (and, if needed, $\rho$) for each run. Second, the simple projected-area blockage ratio reaches about 4.5\% for the largest cylinder. A correction or a quantitative argument that it is negligible should be made using the full test-section and mounting geometry. Third, a finite submerged member in a tank can experience end, support, free-surface, and wall influences; the actual support arrangement and distances to boundaries are needed to assess these effects. Finally, the force scale depends directly on calibration sensitivity and reference length. Calibration uncertainty, tare stability, balance repeatability, and the basis for the 0.60 m wetted length should be reported.

The present series contains one run at each diameter--speed condition. Consequently, temporal force fluctuations within a tow cannot substitute for repeated measurements of run-to-run variability. A useful follow-up would repeat selected speeds, especially the 0.8 m s$^{-1}$ 50.8 mm condition, and interleave cylinder sizes within one session after checking load-cell zero and the carriage speed. This would separate repeatability from date, temperature, and calibration effects.

\section{Conclusions}
Thirty towing-tank runs were reduced to mean drag coefficients for three nominally smooth circular cylinders. Using the current 20 °C property assumption and nominal 0.60 m wetted length, the mean coefficients are 1.144 (25.4 mm), 1.153 (38.1 mm), and 1.178 (50.8 mm), over nominal Reynolds numbers from $5.1\times10^3$ to $5.6\times10^4$. Each series is approximately flat over its tested range. These values provide a useful preliminary result, but the comparison across diameters is not controlled, and the coefficients have not been corrected for blockage, temperature, or finite-length and free-surface effects. The flagged run 17 modestly elevates the 50.8 mm mean; excluding it as a sensitivity check gives 1.159. Temperature-corrected properties, uncertainty propagation, geometric documentation, and repeat measurements are required before drawing stronger conclusions.

\section*{Data and code availability}
Raw time histories, calibration records, the run log, processed forces, and analysis code are included in the accompanying Calkit project. A permanent archival identifier and public release information should be added when the project is ready for publication.

\bibliographystyle{plainnat}
\bibliography{references}
\end{document}

