The load cell was calibrated by hanging known masses and fitting voltage as a linear function of force. The September 2025 calibration gave a sensitivity of approximately 0.020 V N$^{-1}$ in the original processing notes; a new mounting and calibration in March 2026 gave approximately 0.0213 V N$^{-1}$. For each run, voltage recorded before 1 s was averaged to form the tare. The force history was calculated as
\begin{equation}
  F(t)=\frac{V(t)-\overline{V}_{t<1\,\mathrm{s}}}{S},
\end{equation}
where $S$ is the campaign sensitivity. The reported run mean and temporal standard deviation were evaluated over $3<t<8$ s. This standard deviation describes variation within one tow; it is not an estimate of between-run repeatability. The October and November data were recorded as comma-separated files and processed with the September calibration; the March data used semicolon-separated files and the March sensitivity. Run 17 (50.8 mm cylinder, 0.8 m s$^{-1}$) is marked in the log as having a possibly anomalous post-run zero. It is retained in the reported analysis, but its influence is noted below.

The drag coefficient and Reynolds number were calculated using
\begin{equation}
  C_D=\frac{\overline{F}}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho U^2 D L}, \qquad
  Re_D=\frac{UD}{\nu},
\end{equation}
where $U$ is the logged tow speed, $D$ is cylinder diameter, and $L=0.60$ m is the nominal wetted length. The notebook used $\rho=998$ kg m$^{-3}$ and $\nu=1.004\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$ s$^{-1}$, corresponding to water at 20 °C, for every campaign. Consequently, the Reynolds numbers below are nominal values and should be recomputed using the logged temperatures in a final analysis. No blockage, support, free-surface, or end-effect correction was applied. Based on the nominal projected area $DL$ and tank cross-section $0.9\times0.75$ m$^2$, the simple area blockage ratio is approximately 2.3--4.5% across the tested diameters; this estimate does not include supports or local boundary effects.

\section{Results}
The nominal Reynolds-number ranges and mean drag coefficients from the notebook are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:summary}. The full dataset is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:cd}. Within each campaign, the coefficients fluctuate around a roughly level value rather than displaying a strong monotonic trend with speed. The mean values range from 1.144 to 1.178 across size series. The within-series standard deviations in Table~\ref{tab:summary} are calculated across the ten rounded per-run coefficients and therefore combine measurement scatter and any systematic trend; they are not uncertainty intervals on the means.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Summary of the existing analysis. Reynolds-number bounds use $\nu$ at 20 °C. The standard deviation is across the ten per-run $C_D$ values in each size series.}
\label{tab:summary}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Cylinder diameter & $Re_D$ range & Mean $C_D$ & SD of $C_D$ & Number of runs \\
\midrule
25.4 mm & $5.06\times10^3$--$2.78\times10^4$ & 1.144 & 0.032 & 10 \\
38.1 mm & $7.59\times10^3$--$4.17\times10^4$ & 1.153 & 0.026 & 10 \\
50.8 mm & $1.01\times10^4$--$5.57\times10^4$ & 1.178 & 0.070 & 10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The elevated value for run 17 is visible in the 50.8 mm series ($C_D=1.357$). Excluding that run solely as a sensitivity check changes the 50.8 mm series mean from 1.178 to 1.159. The log's drift note is not sufficient by itself to establish that this measurement is invalid, so the primary summary retains it. A repeat tow and inspection of the raw pre-run and post-run zero levels are needed to decide how it should be treated.

